  <h2 id="planktonic-community-structure">Plankton Community Ecology</h2>

  <p>High-frequency plankton imaging makes it possible to study communities as dynamic assemblages that reorganize across water masses, seasons, and years. We examine both how much plankton biomass is present and which organisms make up the community, because changes in abundance and changes in composition can tell different ecological stories.</p>

  <figure class="wide-figure">
  

<img src="/assets/media/ifcb-example-image-sosik-lab-whoi.png" alt="Example Imaging FlowCytobot plankton images from the Sosik Lab at WHOI." />


  
  <figcaption><p>Imaging FlowCytobot (IFCB) images show the morphological diversity of phytoplankton and microplankton. Converting these images into taxonomic and biomass data allows community change to be measured across seasons, years, and water masses. Credit: <a href="https://ifcb-data.whoi.edu/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sosik Lab @ WHOI</a>.</p>
</figcaption>
  
</figure>

  <p>On the Northeast U.S. Shelf, we study spatial, seasonal, and interannual variability in phytoplankton biomass and community composition. Metacommunity analyses help show when local communities change together and when species composition becomes less synchronized. We also use topic-modeling approaches to identify recurring assemblages within complex image-based plankton datasets.</p>

  <div class="theme-gallery paired-gallery">
  
    
<figure>
  

<img src="/assets/media/metacommunity-variability.png" alt="Metacommunity variability figure showing annual and partitioned plankton community signals." />


  
  <figcaption><p>Metacommunity analysis of Imaging FlowCytobot observations separates variability in total phytoplankton biomass from variability in community composition, showing how plankton communities change in both abundance and taxonomic identity across the Northeast U.S. Shelf.</p>
</figcaption>
  
</figure>

  
    
<figure>
  

<img src="/assets/media/ifcb-topic-modeling.png" alt="IFCB topic-modeling figure showing plankton community structure across depth and latitude." />


  
  <figcaption><p>A natural-language-processing approach, similar to Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), represents each plankton sample as a mixture of recurring assemblages. This reveals co-occurrence patterns across depth, latitude, and environmental gradients.</p>
</figcaption>
  
</figure>

  
</div>

  <h2 id="harmful-algal-blooms">Harmful Algal Blooms</h2>

  <p>In collaboration with the Department of Marine Plankton at the Institute of Oceanography, Vietnam, we study potentially harmful and toxin-producing algae in coastal waters. This work connects seasonal field observations, coastal habitats, microscopy, morphology, phylogeny, and culture experiments to improve species identification and understand when harmful taxa occur.</p>

  <figure class="wide-figure">
  

<img src="/assets/media/toxic-algae.png" alt="Microscopic imagery of potentially harmful algal species." />


  
  <figcaption><p>Microscopy, morphology, DNA sequencing, and culture experiments help distinguish potentially harmful algal species that can look similar under the microscope. This work improves identification of toxin-producing taxa in coastal Vietnamese waters. Credit: Department of Marine Plankton, Institute of Oceanography, Vietnam.</p>
</figcaption>
  
</figure>

  <h2 id="ocean-technologies">Ocean Observing Technologies</h2>

  <p>Modern ocean observing systems collect detailed physical, chemical, and biological measurements, but those observations become most useful when they can be viewed together. We develop data systems that connect mobile-platform measurements, plankton imagery, remote sensing, and modeled ocean data so research teams can interpret biological patterns in context.</p>

  <section class="subtheme-card">
  <h3>Mobile Observing Data Systems</h3>
  <p>We build data-processing pipelines and interactive dashboards for observations from tow sleds, autonomous vehicles, and shipboard sensor systems. These tools bring together sensor streams, vehicle tracks, deployment metadata, and imagery so field teams can review data quality, explore water-column structure, and identify features of interest during and after cruises. See the <a href="https://stingraydash.whoi.edu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stingray dashboard</a>.</p>

</section>

  <figure class="wide-figure">
  

<img src="/assets/media/tow-sled-data-integration.png" alt="Data integration workflow for tow-sled ocean observations." />


  
  <figcaption><p>The tow-sled data workflow integrates sensor records, image data, vehicle tracks, and deployment metadata into a shared timeline. This makes it possible to explore mobile-platform observations in their physical and biological context.</p>
</figcaption>
  
</figure>

  <section class="subtheme-card">
  <h3>Machine Learning for Plankton Imaging</h3>
  <p>We develop end-to-end machine-learning and computer-vision workflows for plankton image data. These workflows help transform large image collections into ecological information about abundance, distribution, community composition, and change through time.</p>

</section>

